<h3>CHAPTER 1: Fundamental Physics, Hounsfield Units & Image Reconstruction</h3>

<p>Computed Tomography relies on X-ray attenuation measurements taken from multiple angles around the patient to reconstruct cross-sectional images. Understanding the physical principles of attenuation and density quantification is essential for accurate tissue characterization.</p>

<h4>1.1 Hounsfield Units (HU) & Attenuation Scale</h4>
<p>The Hounsfield scale quantitatively measures tissue radiodensity relative to distilled water (0 HU) and air (-1000 HU). Tissue attenuation values dictate windowing setup and pathology identification.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Tissue / Substance</th>
      <th>Hounsfield Unit (HU) Range</th>
      <th>Clinical Significance</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Air</strong></td>
      <td>-1000 HU</td>
      <td>Lungs, paranasal sinuses, bowel gas, pneumoperitoneum.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Fat</strong></td>
      <td>-120 to -90 HU</td>
      <td>Subcutaneous fat, mesenteric fat stranding in inflammation.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Water</strong></td>
      <td>0 HU (Reference)</td>
      <td>Simple cysts, CSF, simple fluid collections.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Soft Tissue / Muscles</strong></td>
      <td>+30 to +60 HU</td>
      <td>Liver, spleen, muscle, solid abdominal organs.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Acute Blood (Clot)</strong></td>
      <td>+50 to +80 HU</td>
      <td>Hyperdense sign in acute intracranial hemorrhage or vascular thrombosis.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Intravenous Contrast</strong></td>
      <td>+150 to +300+ HU</td>
      <td>Arterial/venous enhancement, active extravasation.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Cortical Bone</strong></td>
      <td>+400 to +1000+ HU</td>
      <td>Skeletal structures, calcifications, metallic hardware.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h4>1.2 Pitch, Slice Thickness & Spatial Resolution</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Pitch Factor:</strong> Defined as table movement per 360° gantry rotation divided by total collimation beam width.
    <ul>
      <li><em>Pitch = 1.0:</em> Standard image quality and dose balance.</li>
      <li><em>Pitch &gt; 1.0:</em> Faster scan coverage, reduced dose, minor decrease in spatial resolution (ideal for trauma / uncooperative patients).</li>
      <li><em>Pitch &lt; 1.0:</em> High spatial resolution, increased radiation dose (cardiac CTA and temporal bone protocols).</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Slice Thickness vs. Interval:</strong> Thin acquisition slices (&le;0.625 mm) optimize multiplanar reformations (MPR) and 3D volume rendering, while thicker reconstructed slices (3–5 mm) reduce visual noise for routine soft tissue reading.</li>
</ul>

