<h3>CHAPTER 3: Contrast Media Dynamics, Timing & Injection Protocols</h3>

<p>Intravenous iodinated contrast media enhances intravascular structures and parenchymal tissue differential attenuation. Precise timing based on cardiac output and injection rate is critical for diagnostic CTA and multiphasic abdominal CT.</p>

<h4>3.1 Contrast Timing Techniques</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Bolus Tracking (Automated Peak Triggering):</strong> A ROI (Region of Interest) locator is placed on a reference vessel (e.g., Abdominal Aorta). Scan acquisition automatically triggers when density reaches a predefined threshold (typically +100 to +150 HU above baseline).</li>
  <li><strong>Test Bolus Technique:</strong> A small test volume (10–15 mL) is injected while taking low-dose monitoring slices at 1–2 second intervals to calculate exact individual peak contrast arrival time.</li>
</ul>

<h4>3.2 Multiphasic Abdominal CT Timing</h4>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Phase Name</th>
      <th>Scan Delay (Post-Injection)</th>
      <th>Primary Clinical Indications</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Early Arterial Phase</strong></td>
      <td>15 to 20 seconds</td>
      <td>Bronchial/thoracic CTA, renal artery mapping.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Late Arterial (Parenchymal) Phase</strong></td>
      <td>35 to 45 seconds</td>
      <td>Hypervascular liver tumors (HCC), pancreatic adenocarcinoma, active arterial bleeding.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Portal Venous Phase</strong></td>
      <td>60 to 70 seconds</td>
      <td>Standard routine abdomen/pelvis, liver parenchyma enhancement, portal vein thrombosis.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Nephrographic Phase</strong></td>
      <td>80 to 100 seconds</td>
      <td>Renal parenchymal mass evaluation and corticomedullary differentiation.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Delayed / Excretory Phase</strong></td>
      <td>3 to 15 minutes</td>
      <td>Urothelial tumors, renal collecting system, scar tissue vs. active leak.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

