<h3>CHAPTER 4: Acute Brain CT: Stroke, Hemorrhage & Trauma Readout</h3>

<p>Non-contrast Head CT (NCCT) is the first-line imaging modality in acute neurological emergencies to rule out intracranial hemorrhage before thrombolytic therapy or surgical intervention.</p>

<h4>4.1 Acute Intracranial Hemorrhage Classification</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Epidural Hematoma (EDH):</strong> Biconvex (lenticular) hyperdense collection bounded by cranial sutures. Often associated with skull fractures and middle meningeal artery rupture.</li>
  <li><strong>Subdural Hematoma (SDH):</strong> Crescentic hyperdense collection crossing suture lines, bounded by dural reflections. Associated with bridging vein rupture.</li>
  <li><strong>Subarachnoid Hemorrhage (SAH):</strong> Hyperdense attenuation filling the basal cisterns, sulci, and Sylvian fissures. Ruptured berry aneurysm is the primary etiology.</li>
  <li><strong>Intraparenchymal Hemorrhage (IPH):</strong> Hyperdense clot within the brain parenchyma, accompanied by surrounding hypodense cytotoxic edema and mass effect.</li>
</ul>

<h4>4.2 Early Ischemic Stroke Signs (The "ASPECTS" Focus)</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Hyperdense Middle Cerebral Artery (MCA) Sign:</strong> Acute intravascular thrombus within the M1 segment (+70 to +80 HU).</li>
  <li><strong>Loss of Insular Ribbon:</strong> Loss of grey-white matter differentiation in the insular cortex due to cytotoxic edema.</li>
  <li><strong>Obscuration of Lentiform Nucleus:</strong> Hypodensity and loss of sharp borders in the basal ganglia within 3–6 hours of ischemic onset.</li>
</ul>

<div class="illustration-box">
  <strong>[Illustration 4.1 Placement & Prompt]</strong><br/>
